{{$NEXT}}

  ** Thanks to Giovanni <g.bechis@snb.it> for these changes:
  - RFC 7208 says there MUST be a limit of no more than 10 MX records
    looked up and checked; throw a permerror when the limit is exceeded
    instead of silently truncating the list
  - RFC 7208 says implementations SHOULD limit "void lookups" to two;
    enable the corresponding regression tests

  - Ensure every module's $VERSION is set by Dist::Zilla at release time,
    and manage the version number in README the same way

  - Security: escape untrusted text in the Received-SPF header to
    prevent header field injection:
    - escape parentheses and backslashes in the explanation comment,
      so text from a malformed SPF record or the sender identity can
      no longer close the comment early and inject forged fields such
      as client-ip=
    - escape control characters (e.g. CR/LF) in the envelope-from and
      helo fields
  - Fix macro expansion stopping at the first line break in a macro
    string, leaving later macros unexpanded
  - Security: authority_explanation now escapes control and non-ASCII
    characters in the explanation string (exp= TXT record) published in
    DNS, and document that the result text() may contain untrusted data
  - Remove the check for includes of the same domain through CNAME
    aliases. Including a domain and a CNAME alias of it is legal in
    RFC 7208, but the check tried to throw a hard fail for it. With
    a real resolver it read an unpopulated Net::DNS internal field and
    so in practice did nothing, but where it did take effect (e.g.
    with Net::DNS::Resolver::Programmable) it could wrongly fail
    authorized senders, and it used CNAME targets from DNS as
    unescaped regular expressions, which could make process() die.
    It also made an extra CNAME lookup per include that was not
    counted towards the RFC 7208 DNS lookup and void lookup limits.
    The test suite's resolver now follows CNAMEs, which is what the
    RFC 7208 cname-aliasing test case needed
  - Security: an exception thrown by the DNS resolver, including
    Net::DNS refusing to compose a query for an invalid domain name,
    made the SPF result none, i.e. no policy, rather than temperror.
    As invalid names can come from macros expanded with the sender's
    local part or HELO name, a sender could turn a domain's -all
    policy into none. Invalid domain names (empty label, label longer
    than 63 bytes, or name longer than 253 bytes) are now treated as
    non-existent without querying the resolver, per RFC 7208, and
    other resolver exceptions result in temperror
  - Check the IP address version in the ip6 mechanism using the public
    NetAddr::IP API, rather than enumerating the request's address via
    an overloaded array dereference and reading an internal field,
    which died if the request's IP address was a large network
  - Allow Mail::SPF::Server->process() to evaluate a proposed SPF record
    passed in via a new 'record' option, instead of querying DNS for the
    authority domain's record, so a not-yet-published record can be tested
    against real DNS state

  - Prefer SPF1 over SPF2 (Sender ID, which is deprecated) when a record
    could be parsed as either

  - When a domain publishes both "v=spf1" and "spf2.0" records, always
    select the "v=spf1" record. Previously the record was chosen by the
    order of the records in the DNS answer, so the result could vary
    between queries, although the documentation and RFC 4406 said
    "spf2.0" records were preferred. Sender ID is historic (RFC 6686),
    so "v=spf1" is now deliberately preferred
  - Security: a "p" macro in an explanation string (exp= TXT record)
    made a PTR look-up and up to 10 A/AAAA look-ups for every
    occurrence, not limited by the RFC 7208 DNS look-up limits, so a
    sender could make a single SPF check send thousands of DNS
    queries. The "p" macro value is now looked up at most once per SPF
    check and authority domain. Also, exceeding the void look-up limit
    while expanding the explanation made authority_explanation() die;
    it now falls back to the default explanation
  - Reject a network (e.g. '10.0.0.0/8') as a request's ip_address
    with an EInvalidOptionValue exception. NetAddr::IP accepted it,
    which made no sense for SPF and changed the meaning of the
    mechanisms' address checks
  - Ignore an explanation string (exp= TXT record) that contains
    anything other than printable ASCII characters and spaces, as
    RFC 7208 requires, and use the default explanation instead.
    Control characters were already escaped, but an MTA using the
    explanation in an SMTP reply would still have sent the escaped
    text of a malicious explanation
  - Return none without querying DNS when the identity's domain is
    empty (e.g. "MAIL FROM:<a@>", which queried the root zone) or a
    single-label name (e.g. "HELO mailhost", which the resolver may
    expand with its search list and so query the receiver's internal
    domains), as RFC 7208 requires
  - Security: spfd now escapes control characters in the values of
    its response, so text from DNS or the client cannot add lines to
    the response, and in its debug output

